.TH QUADRF.CONF 5 "August 2026" "QuadRF" "QuadRF"
.SH NAME
quadrf.conf \- site configuration for a QuadRF appliance
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I /etc/quadrf/quadrf.conf
.SH DESCRIPTION
Plain shell assignments, read by the maintainer scripts of the quadrf-*
packages, by the runtime helpers under
.I /usr/lib/quadrf
and by
.BR quadrf (1).
Nothing else may appear in the file: it is sourced, not parsed.
.PP
Run
.B quadrf apply
after editing. Settings that change the firmware configuration also need a
reboot, which
.B quadrf status
will report.
.SH SETTINGS
.TP
.B QUADRF_USER
Account that owns the desktop session, the web control panel and the demo
applications. The packages ship generic systemd units and override the user
through a drop-in, so changing this and running
.B quadrf apply
moves the services to another account. Default:
.BR dietpi .
.TP
.B QUADRF_BOOT_DIR
Firmware partition holding
.IR config.txt ,
.I cmdline.txt
and the
.I overlays/
directory. Default:
.IR /boot/firmware .
.B QUADRF_HOSTNAME
mDNS and nginx name. Clients reach the appliance at
.IR HOSTNAME.local .
The locally generated server certificate uses the same name; changing it and
running
.B quadrf apply
issues a replacement from the existing local root CA.
A second unit on the LAN takes
.IR HOSTNAME-2
unless
.B QUADRF_HOSTNAME_LOCK
is yes. The remote desktop is also published as
.IR HOSTNAME-desktop.local
and
.IR HOSTNAMEd.local
(single-label mDNS names;
.IR quadrfd.local
and
.IR quadrfD.local
are the same name). Browsers do not resolve
.IR desktop.HOSTNAME.local .
Default:
.BR quadrf .
.TP
.B QUADRF_HOSTNAME_LOCK
.BR yes
stops automatic rename when another unit holds the same mDNS name.
The joining unit is the one that becomes
.IR HOSTNAME-2 ;
the unit that already has the name keeps it. The Network Setup page
shows this control when the name is not the factory
.BR quadrf .
Default:
.BR no .
.TP
.B QUADRF_AP_SSID
SSID of the fallback access point. Default:
.BR QuadRF .
.TP
.B QUADRF_AP_PASS
WPA2 passphrase for the fallback access point. Empty (the default) leaves the
AP open. Set from the control panel or with
.BR quadrf-apply-ap (8).
.TP
.B QUADRF_AP_ADDRESS
Address the appliance takes on wlan0 in access point mode, and the target of
the wlan0-only captive DNS wildcard. A /24 is assumed. Default:
.BR 192.168.44.1 .
.TP
.B QUADRF_WIFI_MODE
Last operator choice for wlan0:
.BR sta
(try a saved client network; the control panel labels this Client),
.BR ap
(hotspot immediately), or
.BR off
(wlan0 down). Empty means
.BR sta .
Written by the control panel toggles; a failed client join that starts the
fallback AP does not change this. Default:
.BR sta .
.TP
.B QUADRF_WIFI_FALLBACK
.BR yes
(the default) starts the hotspot when a client join fails.
.BR no
leaves wlan0 without an AP. The control panel exposes this as
.RI "If client fails, start hotspot" .
.TP
.B QUADRF_OPENOCD
OpenOCD binary used to program the FPGA. When empty the first of
.I /usr/lib/quadrf/openocd/bin/openocd
or
.I /usr/bin/openocd
is used.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
QUADRF_USER=operator
QUADRF_BOOT_DIR=/boot/firmware
QUADRF_HOSTNAME=quadrf-14
QUADRF_HOSTNAME_LOCK=no
QUADRF_AP_SSID=QuadRF-14
QUADRF_AP_PASS=
QUADRF_AP_ADDRESS=192.168.44.1
QUADRF_WIFI_MODE=sta
QUADRF_WIFI_FALLBACK=yes
QUADRF_OPENOCD=
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR quadrf (1),
.BR quadrf-apply-hostname (8)
